{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the birth of facebook and the turbulent personal and legal battles that surrounded it. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a sharp, fast-paced exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant but emotionally detached genius, driven by a need for validation and a sense of exclusion. his performance is nuanced, with every twitch and pause revealing layers of insecurity masked by arrogance. the supporting cast is equally compelling: andrew garfield as eduardo saverin brings heart and vulnerability to the story, while jesse plemons and josh pelham add depth as the winklevoss twins, whose portrayal is both eerie and oddly sympathetic.\n\naaron sorkin’s script is a tour de force—dense with dialogue that crackles with wit and intellectual tension. every line feels deliberate, every exchange a battle of wits. the courtroom scenes, intercut with flashbacks, create a compelling narrative structure that keeps the viewer engaged even as the story unfolds across multiple timelines.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is meticulous. the film’s visual tone is cold and sleek, mirroring the sterile world of silicon valley and the emotional detachment of its characters. the use of light, shadow, and framing reinforces the isolation of zuckerberg, even in crowded rooms. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is haunting and minimalist, perfectly complementing the film’s mood with electronic pulses that echo the rhythm of code and the heartbeat of a new era.\n\nwhat makes the social network so compelling is that it doesn’t try to paint anyone as a hero or villain. instead, it presents a web of motivations, misunderstandings, and missed opportunities. the film raises questions about ownership, friendship, and the ethics of innovation without offering easy answers.\n\nthe pacing never falters, and even though the subject matter could easily become dry, sorkin’s dialogue and fincher’s rhythm turn it into a thriller. by the end, you’re left not just with a story about a website, but with a haunting portrait of modern success—how it’s built on broken relationships and the quiet loneliness of those who reach the top but have no one to share it with.\n\nthe social network is more than a movie about facebook. it’s a timeless meditation on power, connection, and what we sacrifice in the pursuit of legacy. it’s smart, it’s gripping, and it’s unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0004889961564913392}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.31129345297813416}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.47485053539276123}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.001073380233719945}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0032333259005099535}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011812942830147222}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -0.8836938738822937}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.4305012882687151e-05}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -1.9656167030334473}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0017058596713468432}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0005032941699028015}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.1370375156402588}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.490105023549404e-05}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -0.8423704504966736}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.2519221007823944}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.03638831526041031}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -0.018351059406995773}, {"token": " battles", "logprob": -0.07758452743291855}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0